Python ≥[[ python_min ]].

## Release phase
[[ release_phase ]]. In mvp, only blockers are fixed inside a PR: the main path breaks on realistic
input; private data leaks on realistic input; a hard-rule violation; irreversible loss or overwrite of
the user's existing data (source files, history, remote repos) on a plausible invocation, even a
mistaken one, when a cheap guard would prevent it; or a test that falsely passes. Everything else is
filed as an `improvement` issue. Change this line to `hardening` before a first formal release.

## Real data
[% if real_data_access == "allowed" -%]
Real data: allowed. Agents may read real data on the maintainer's machine when a task needs it.
[%- elif real_data_access == "with-approval" -%]
Real data: with-approval. Agents may read real data on the maintainer's machine only after the
maintainer approves one named data source on the issue or PR, for that issue or PR only.
[%- else -%]
Real data: never. Agents do not read real user data. The maintainer runs shapes-only scripts (record
types, field names, counts, never values) and replies with the output.
[%- endif %]

At every level: never commit real data, and never quote values from it in issues, PRs or commits (field
names, counts and shapes only). Tests use synthetic fixtures only. Read real data where it lives; never
copy it into the repo or worktree (use a scratch directory outside the repo). A stricter hard rule in
this file overrides the level.

## Where knowledge lives
- **GitHub and the repo are the source of truth:** issues, PRs, review threads, labels, plans and
  implementation notes in `.factory/designs/`, and this file. Decisions and their reasons go here, where
  the maintainer and every agent can see them.
- **Each PR's "## Handoff" section** says what changed and why, the key decisions, how to test, and
  what's open. Keep it current after every change, so the next agent or reviewer starts from it.
- **The factory run log** (`.factory/runs/`, gitignored) is a local cache and cost ledger. It points at
  PRs, commits and threads and never holds decisions.
- **Questions for the maintainer** go on the PR as inline review comments, with options and a
  recommendation. Work continues elsewhere until they're answered.

## Models
The factory skill (`.claude/skills/factory/`) runs in Claude Code or GitHub Copilot (the CLI or the app)
and gives each role its own model. Pass the role's model explicitly whenever you spawn it; the names
differ by harness.

| Role | Claude Code | GitHub Copilot |
|---|---|---|
| **Orchestrator**: your session; plans lanes, checks plans, runs the gates, merges | `opus` | `claude-opus-5.5` |
| **Builder**: plans, builds and fixes one issue; also the resume builder and issue writers | `sonnet` | `claude-sonnet-5.5` |
| **Reviewer**: the design reviewer and the code reviewer, fresh for each issue | `opus` | `claude-opus-5.5` |

`/model` shows and changes the session's model in both. To use other models, the maintainer edits this
table. An organization's policy can limit the models on offer: if a role's model isn't available, use the
closest one that is and say so in the run summary.[% if claude_actions %] The Actions-mode workflows set their own model
(`agent_model` in `.copier-answers.yml`).[% elif copilot_actions %] The Actions-mode workflows set their own model
(`copilot_model` in `.copier-answers.yml`).[% endif %]

## Setup
uv sync --locked --extra dev    # same lockfile CI uses

## Definition of "checks pass" (identical to .github/workflows/ci.yml; run all, in order)
1. uvx ruff@[[ ruff_version ]] format --check .         # ruff pinned to match CI
2. uvx ruff@[[ ruff_version ]] check .
3. uv run --no-sync pytest -q               # offline: no network, no model, no real data

If ci.yml changes, update this list to match. Never "fix" a failure by
changing lint rules, the ruff pin, or skipping tests.

## Hard rules
[% if extra_hard_rules | trim %][[ extra_hard_rules | trim ]]
[% endif %]- Never commit secrets, credentials, personal data or private datasets.
- Never upload anything to an external service (package indexes, dataset hubs,
  …), and never ask for or use credentials beyond what the workflows provide.
- Never call paid model endpoints. Code that needs an LLM takes an injectable
  client, and tests use a fake one.
- New dependencies only when the approved design lists them. Heavy or optional
  ones go behind an extra, and the base install must work without them.
- Never bump the version, publish a release, or edit release tooling unless the
  approved design says so explicitly.
- Never touch .github/workflows/ unless the approved design says so explicitly.
  Such changes go in a PR of their own[% if claude_actions %]: agent stages skip on a PR that modifies
  their workflow, and the change only takes effect after merge[% elif copilot_actions %]. The Actions-mode
  agents can't push them: the workflow token can't change .github/workflows/[% endif %].
- Every code change ships its docs in the same PR: new docs for new behaviour,
  and updates to any doc it makes wrong or stale (README, CHANGELOG,
  docstrings, `.factory/designs/`). Docs that disagree with the PR's code break
  this rule, so they're a blocker even in `mvp`. Mismatches outside the PR's
  diff aren't fixed in it: file them as sub-issues of the "Documentation
  tech-debt" issue.

## Style
- Match existing code style; ruff is the formatter of record.
- Small, reviewable diffs. No drive-by refactors.
- Type hints everywhere.
